<p>Seeking Greenlight: Yes</p>
<p>Page Type: SCP Article</p>
<p>Genre: Happy(?)</p>
<p>Page Layout: Standard article with a death report and psychological analysis, and maybe a interview log</p>
<p>Elevator Pitch:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>•The SCP consists of two parts, POI-628356 and SCP-XXXX</p>
<p>•SCP-XXXX Is a dog formerly named &quot;Thunder&quot;, who was previously owned by the POI-628356. According to the article, POI-628356 is the 15 year old son of A Researcher at a random site (Still haven't decided) who, as mentioned above, owned SCP-XXXX</p>
<p>•SCP-XXXX had to be put down at a vet clinic due to complications from a brain tumor.</p>
<p>•The SCP itself is anomalous because despite being &quot;Dead&quot; It still exists and acts like a completely normal dog. It eats, poops, pees, drinks and can even be petted. However, The SCP is completely invisible to everyone Except POI-628356 and other dogs.</p>
<p>•The whole concept behind the SCP is the fact that despite being dead, SCP-XXXX is in a quite literal sense, always with The Person of interest. This article is more about the story than the SCP.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Central Narrative:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The Central narrative follows the aftermath of Thunder's death and the effect it has on POI-628356, as well as the results of seeing his dog &quot;alive&quot; again. The Psychological analysis and vet clinic report will be the main focus of the article, with the possible interview log between POI-628356 being a sort of closing addition to how much thunder meant to POI-628356 and how happy he is to be able to see his dog again, even if no one else can.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Hook/Attention-Grabber:
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The Article is meant to be a happy and sad SCP article, and is mainly to play into the idea that &quot;They're only gone if you think they are&quot; In a literal sense.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

